01:37.040 --> 01:39.640
When you allow your code base to start

01:39.640 --> 01:43.520
messing with that source of truth in different places,

01:43.520 --> 01:45.360
all of a sudden it becomes a lot trickier to

01:45.360 --> 01:47.800
figure out why and when something happened.

01:47.800 --> 01:49.640
Whereas if you follow this pattern of

01:49.640 --> 01:51.640
emitting for children to parent,

01:51.640 --> 01:54.200
you'll always know that if in the future the user has

01:54.200 --> 01:57.120
a bug where this list, for example, is broken,

01:57.120 --> 01:59.120
you'll know that basically you probably should be looking at

01:59.120 --> 02:03.320
the parent and basically you'll probably be able to solve it just off that alone.

02:03.320 --> 02:05.640
But if you allow the mutation of props elsewhere,

02:05.640 --> 02:07.640
all of a sudden that guarantee becomes

02:07.640 --> 02:10.480
far less likely is because you just don't know what's happening.

02:10.480 --> 02:13.720
So I always err on the side of keeping it easy when it comes

02:13.720 --> 02:16.960
to maintenance and debugging.

